<p id="dna-s-product"></p>

<small>Each bar is the weight of this chapter in a country's `r sp("dna-flow", flow_word)`; the dashed amber line is `r focus_name`'s.
The three closest countries are in blue, and the label says how far each one is, in percentage points.</small>

#### Method

For each country $c$ and year $t$, let $s_{c,i,t}$ be the share of HS2 chapter $i$ in total exports (or imports) to the world.

$$FK(LU, c, t) = \sum_i \min\big(s_{LU,i,t},\; s_{c,i,t}\big)$$

**Product view.** The sum can be split by chapter: chapter $i$ contributes $\min(s_{LU,i}, s_{c,i})$, so the contributions
add up exactly to $FK(LU, c)$. The butterfly chart shows these terms for one country; the heatmap shows them for the five
closest twins. Chapters outside the ten largest are folded into one "Other chapters" row so the totals stay exact.

**Product picker.** For one chapter $i$, the gap $|s_{LU,i} - s_{c,i}|$ says how far country $c$ is from Luxembourg on that
product, and countries are ranked by it. It is the chapter's own term in the distance behind the score:
$\sum_i |s_{LU,i} - s_{c,i}| = 2\,(1 - FK)$.

Finger, J.M. & Kreinin, M.E. (1979), *A measure of export similarity and its possible uses*, Economic Journal 89.

